<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ded Moroz</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Morozko, Father Frost, Grandfather Frost, Old Man Frost</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ded Moroz, whose name is traditionally translated as Father Frost, is a legendary figure of East Slavic tradition similar to Father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-claus">Santa Claus</a>. His origins predate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> as a Slavic personification of winter, and since the 19th century his legend has been shaped by literature, among it Aleksandr Ostrovsky's play The Snow Maiden. He is depicted with a long white beard, a heel-length fur coat and a magic stick, bringing presents at New Year with his granddaughter Snegurochka. In 1928 he was declared an ally of the priest and kulak, but traditions were revived after a letter published in Pravda in 1935, and he became the main symbol of the Soviet New Year's holiday.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ded-moroz.jpg" alt="Ded Moroz" width="309" height="500" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ded_Moroz_Snegurochka_Christmas_card.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The character has roots in Slavic mythology and is also known as Morozko; the literal translation of the name is Grandfather Frost. Since the 19th century his legend has been shaped by literary influences, themselves influenced by the Western tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-claus">Santa Claus</a>. Ostrovsky's play The Snow Maiden was influential, as was Rimsky-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korsakov-sakhalin">Korsakov</a>'s opera based on it, and by the end of the century Ded Moroz was a popular figure. The children's custom of writing letters to him dates from then.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Ded Moroz wears a heel-length fur coat, in red or blue, a semi-round fur hat and valenki, and has a long white beard. He walks with a long magic stick and often rides a troika. He brings presents to well-mannered children, in person in the days of December and secretly under the New Year tree overnight on New Year's Eve, accompanied by Snegurochka, the Snow Maiden, his granddaughter and helper. In 1998 Yury Luzhkov, then Mayor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliky-ustyug">Veliky Ustyug</a> in Vologda Oblast to be his home, and between 2003 and 2010 its post office received about 2,000,000 letters for him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> traditions were discouraged as bourgeois and religious, yet Ded Moroz took his present form in Soviet times and became the main symbol of the New Year holiday that replaced Christmas. Pavel Postyshev's letter in Pravda of 28 December 1935 argued that the holiday's pre-Christian origins mattered less than the benefits it could bring to Soviet children.</p><p>Equivalents spread across the Eastern bloc. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> he replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-nicholas">Saint Nicholas</a>, of whom Soviet authorities disapproved for his Christian origin. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, after the Communists decided in 1948 that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> should not be marked, Moş Gerilă, &quot;Old Man Frosty&quot;, replaced Moş Crăciun and was said to bring gifts on 31 December. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a> public processions of Dedek Mraz began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ljubljana">Ljubljana</a> in 1953. After the end of communism Moş Crăciun replaced Moş Gerilă in Romania, and Dyado Koleda again became more popular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ded Moroz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its gift-bringer was <b>Ded Moroz</b>, Father Frost: declared in 1928 an ally of the priest and the kulak, he was revived after a letter published in Pravda in 1935 and became the main symbol of the Soviet New Year's holiday, bringing his presents at New Year.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ded Moroz</b>, Father Frost, is a figure of East Slavic tradition whose origins predate Christianity as a Slavic personification of winter, and whose legend has been shaped by literature since the 19th century.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li></ul></section>
